  "findings": [
    "Build-guided repair cost 3 API calls across four subjects and turned two unscoreable artifacts into scoreable ones. commons-validator went from 8 collection errors to a clean 382/462 in a single call, without ever seeing a test.",
    "Two subjects consumed zero calls: commons-cli and commons-fileupload already had every module importing, so a build-guided arm has nothing to act on. That is the correct outcome, not a failure.",
    "The arms converge on commons-csv (185 vs 189) but diverge sharply on commons-cli (110 vs 370). commons-cli's dominant failure is an AttributeError raised at runtime, which a parse/import check structurally cannot see. In a statically typed target it would be a compile error; in Python it is not. That is a real limit of compiler-guided repair on a dynamically typed language.",
    "Repair is not universally effective. commons-fileupload survived three test-guided iterations, five files changed, with no change in its score at all.",
    "Even with 12 test-guided calls and full oracle visibility, no repaired arm reaches CodeWeaver on any subject."
  ],
  "caveats": [
    "N=1 per run; no repetitions and no variance.",
    "The test arm read the oracle's failure output. It is not comparable to any test-blind arm and must never be tabulated beside one without stating the difference.",
    "commons-csv still has one collection error in both arms, so neither is a clean full-suite pass rate.",
    "The loop keeps the FINAL iteration, as CRUST-bench does, not the best-scoring one. Per-iteration trajectories are recorded so regressions are visible rather than hidden.",
    "Cost is provider-reported tokens only; no premium-request or AIU figure is inferred."
  ]
}
